{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The desire that is stirred is sweet to the soul, but turning away from evil is an abomination to fools (Proverbs 13:19).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, upon him be peace, warns in this verse (Proverbs 13) about a person, that he should not be drawn after desires but rather subdue them and remove them from himself. For whoever removes them from himself and masters over them — lo, he is in the rank of an angel; and whoever makes his desires overpower him — lo, he is in the rank of a beast. For we find in the work of Creation that all existents, on the sixth day before man was created, were in two categories: intelligible and earthly. The intelligible ones are the supernal created beings, which are pure intellect, separate from matter; the earthly ones are the trees and plants and other living creatures, which are sheer matter with no intellect in them. And these two categories perform their designated actions — these through intellect and those through nature — and each is compelled in its action, this one through its intellect and that one through its nature, and none of them has free choice. Therefore Wisdom was required to create on the sixth day a third being, compounded and combined from both of them, encompassing both intellect and nature — intellect like the separate intelligences, and nature like the earthly beings — and this is man. And his action was to be both intellectual and natural, not compelled in either part but rather volitional; for the choice and the will are in his hand, to be drawn to whichever side he wishes — either after the intellect or after nature, which is desire. And if he is zealous and goes forth and battles those desires — lo, he is stripped of matter and comes into the rank of an angel. And if he is drawn after them — lo, he is stripped of intellect and comes into the rank of a beast. For so was the first man, before he sinned and desired desire: he was an angel of YHWH of Hosts, entirely pure intellect, beautiful, with no blemish in him. And he was worthy of living eternally like one of the supernal ministers, and he was placed in the earthly Garden of Eden, which is the choicest of places. And once he sinned and was drawn after desires, he was stripped of intellect and clothed in matter, and then he was banished from the Garden and was given grass for his food like a beast — as it is written of him (Genesis 3): ״ואכלת את עשב השדה״ — instead of eating at first the fruits of the Garden, which were the planting of YHWH for splendor. Therefore it is fitting for every person of understanding to break his desire and subdue it, so that the breaking of desire should be a sweet thing to the intellectual soul. And upon this Solomon said here: תאוה נהיה תערב לנפש — the desire that is broken (נהיה, from the root of breaking) is a sweet and pleasant thing to the soul of the understanding person. And the word נהיה is from the language of (Daniel 8): ״נהייתי ונחליתי״, and likewise (Exodus 9): ״הנה יד ה׳ הויה״. And the matter is: it is fitting for the understanding person to remove and eliminate from himself the desire called evil, as stated (Genesis 8): ״כי יצר לב האדם רע מנעוריו״, and written (ibid. 6): ״וכל יצר מחשבות לבו רק רע כל היום״. And since the one who breaks his desire is one who turns aside from evil, therefore it says: ותועבת כסילים סור מרע — for what is sweet to the soul of the understanding person is an abominable thing to the fool, because he is its opposite; this is ותועבת כסילים סור מרע. And know that one who is drawn after desires transgresses the entire Torah, and the being drawn after desires is in four parts: the first in his thought, the second in his speech, the third in his food, the fourth in his body. The first, in his thought: that a person is drawn after his thought in evil musings and evil thoughts. And Solomon, upon him be peace, has already revealed to us, teaching the people knowledge, that a person is punished for the sin of thought as if he had sinned in deed. This is what he said (Proverbs 15): ״לב שמח ייטיב פנים״ — for the joy of the heart, whether in a commandment or in a transgression, is visibly impressed upon the body, because the heart is the root of the body, and the disposition of the root is revealed in the branches — and it is as though he sinned in deed. The second, in his speech: in the sin of evil speech and talebearing and vulgarity of the mouth, for this portion of speech is disgraceful, repugnant, and forbidden by Torah law. And upon this Solomon, upon him be peace, said (ibid.): ״ופי כסילים יביע אולת״, and immediately adjacent to it: ״בכל מקום עיני ה׳ צופות רעים וטובים״ — meaning, He will take note of this speech to punish it. And so Isaiah, upon him be peace, prophesied in his rebukes and said (Isaiah 9): ״וכל פה דובר נבלה״, and since the way of young men is with this species of speech, therefore Scripture began (ibid.): ״על כן על בחוריו לא ישמח ה׳״. The third, in his food: that he is drawn after his desire in foods that the Torah has forbidden, or in permitted foods — for even this is forbidden by Torah law. And upon this David, upon him be peace, said (Psalms 40): ״ותורתך בתוך מעי״ — he praised himself that he never placed anything forbidden into his innards, and his food was only in accordance with the Torah. Needless to say he was careful of forbidden foods — but even with permitted foods he was careful against luxurious indulgence, for (Proverbs 13): ״צדיק אוכל לשובע נפשו״, and Scripture said (ibid. 21): ״שומר פיו ולשונו שומר מצרות נפשו״. The fourth, in his body: that he is drawn after his desire in the matter of forbidden relations — and needless to say in what the Torah has forbidden him, for even the permitted, in luxurious excess, is a sin. And here you have four categories in which a person is naturally disposed to be drawn after his desire. And whence do we know that one drawn after desires transgresses the entire Torah? Did not King Solomon reveal this matter to us, saying (Proverbs 18): ״לתאוה יבקש נפרד בכל תושיה יתגלע״ — he says that one who pursues desire and seeks it will find himself נפרד, separated, meaning alone, with no friend or companion left to him, for everyone flees from him and withdraws from him because of his wicked conduct. And lo, this one kicks and shows contempt for the Torah — this is ״בכל תושיה יתגלע״. And because of this we were commanded in the Torah to separate ourselves from desires — and needless to say in forbidden things, for even in permitted things, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: קדש עצמך במותר לך — sanctify yourself in what is permitted to you. And all who separate themselves from desires are called holy, as we find with the nazirite, who separated himself only from wine, yet is called holy, as stated (Numbers 6): ״קדוש יהיה גדל פרע שער ראשו״. And likewise Israel at Sinai were called holy when they received the Torah, as written (Exodus 19): ״ואתם תהיו לי ממלכת כהנים וגוי קדוש״. So that they would separate from desires, they were commanded in the attribute of holiness. And this is what is written." "<b>You shall be holy, for holy am I YHWH your Elohim.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the language of holiness is the matter of separation and distinction. And this verse is an admonition to all Israel that they should be separated and fenced in their character traits — let each and every one reflect upon those who begot him and testify concerning them: let the son testify concerning his father and mother that they are to him like the Creator, and let him testify concerning the Creator, that He is the supernal Father, blessed be He, who created His world anew. This is what it mentions immediately: ״איש אמו ואביו תיראו ואת שבתותי תשמרו״ — a man, his mother and his father, you shall revere — for the commandment of the Sabbath is a testimony and proof of the renewal of the world. And the reason it placed the mother before the father is because the way of children is to be lax in reverence for their mothers, but not so with the father; therefore in the matter of reverence it placed the mother before the father. But in the matter of honor it placed the father before the mother, as written (Exodus 20): ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״. And if both of them were to agree that you should desecrate the Sabbath, therefore it appended: ״ואת שבתותי תשמרו אני ה׳ אלהיכם״ — meaning, they are Mine, belonging to all of you, for you and they are equally obligated in My honor. And immediately it appended idolatry to it, as it says: ״אל תפנו אל האלילים״ — for one who denies the renewal of the world is like an idolater. And the language of אל תפנו is the language of the turning of the heart, and Scripture warns that one should not turn his heart to them and say that they have any reality, but rather they should be in his eyes as vanity and emptiness; and it is like (Deuteronomy 29): ״אשר לבבו פונה היום״, (ibid. 30): ״ואם יפנה לברך ולא תשמע״. And it says: ״לא תעשו לכם״ — that you shall not make them with your hands, even though in your mind you know there is no reality to them. ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם״ — YHWH is faithful to pay reward; אלהיכם — a judge to exact punishment." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret ״קדושים תהיו״ as the holiness of thought, in the manner that they said: a person should always sanctify himself at the time of conjugal relations. And since the perfection of holiness for the seed of Israel depends on formation, therefore the prophet said (Ezekiel 23): ״וגם את בניהן אשר ילדו לי״ — He called Israel those born to Him, for they are designated to His name. And after the section of forbidden relations was mentioned above regarding the prohibited category, he now warns and says ״קדושים תהיו״ regarding the permitted category that is fitting for you to cleave to, for one who cleaves to his kind in holiness — even one who guards himself from forbidden relations — is called holy. And in this admonition of holiness he mentioned all the congregation of the children of Israel, in order to include women as well, for the admonition applies to all, men and women; as written regarding the donation of the Tabernacle (Exodus 35): ״ויקהל משה את כל עדת בני ישראל״ — for the donation was for all Israel, men and women together. And since the father and mother are the cause of the son, therefore it says: ״איש אמו ואביו תיראו״ — for there are three partners in a person: the Holy One, blessed be He, his father, and his mother. And it placed the mother first because of her merit, for holiness is a greater excellence in a woman than in a man. And after the mother it mentioned the father, who is the principal. And after them, the third partner — may the Supernal One be blessed above all. And from this the verse concludes: ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם״ — He is the partner who implants the soul within him, and He is the principal. And it says ״את שבתותי תשמרו״ because the principal time for conjugal union is on the Sabbath, and the word תשמרו is like the language of Scripture (Genesis 37): ״ואביו שמר את הדבר״, and like the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory: שמור לי על הפתח — guard the door for me. And this is what is written (Isaiah 56): ״כי כה אמר ה׳ לסריסים אשר ישמרו את שבתותי״ — to eunuchs, who are those who suppress themselves all the days of the week, אשר ישמרו את שבתותי — for they wait for the Sabbath, which is the principal time for conjugal union, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Psalms 1): ״אשר פריו יתן בעתו״ — this is one who conjoints his bed from Sabbath eve to Sabbath eve. And their reward, which He will give them in the World to Come, is ״יד ושם טוב מבנים ומבנות״ — a hand and a good name, better than sons and daughters that they have in this world." "<b>And there are those</b> who interpreted that the reason it mentioned eunuchs in the commandment of the Sabbath is that the commandment of the Sabbath was commanded for all generations, as stated (Exodus 31): ״לדורותם ברית עולם״ — and eunuchs, since they do not beget children and have no generations, might consider themselves exempt from the Sabbath; therefore it admonished them to keep it, and He will give them a reward better than sons and daughters. And it appended ״אל תפנו״ because it is forbidden to gaze at women, and so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: whoever gazes at women transgresses ״אל תפנו אל האלילים״. And since it is forbidden to think about another woman, therefore it appended to it: ״ואלהי מסכה לא תעשו לכם״ — for whoever conjoints his bed and thinks about another woman, those children are called אלהי מסכה — molten gods." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'You shall be holy' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: Since you have been sanctified for My name before I created the world, be holy just as I am holy, as it is said: 'You shall be holy, for I HaShem am holy.' And the explanation of this is that since Israel was the first thought and they were sanctified before the existence of created being came to be, in the manner that the prophet said (Jeremiah 2): ״קדש ישראל לה׳ ראשית תבואתה״ — 'Israel is holy to HaShem, the first of His produce' — therefore He warns them to be holy after they came into being; this is 'You shall be holy.' To what may the matter be compared? To a king who betrothed a woman and said to her: Since you have been betrothed to my name, I am king and you are queen; just as this is my honor, so it is your honor. Why? Because you are my wife. So the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Go and sanctify Israel, as it is said (Exodus 19): ״לך אל העם וקדשתם היום ומחר״ — 'Go to the people and sanctify them today and tomorrow.' The Holy One, blessed be He, sanctified them and said (ibid.): ״ואתם תהיו לי ממלכת כהנים וגוי קדוש״ — 'And you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.' Why? 'For I HaShem your Elohim am holy' — therefore 'You shall be holy.' R. Levi said: The Ten Commandments are written in this passage. (Exodus 20): ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״, and here it is written ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם״. ״לא יהיה לך״, and here it is written ״ואלהי מסכה״. ״לא תשא״, and here it is written ״ולא תשבעו בשמי״. ״זכור את יום השבת״, and here it is written ״את שבתותי תשמרו״. ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״, and here it is written ״איש אמו ואביו תיראו״. ״לא תרצח״, and here it is written ״לא תעמוד על דם רעך״. ״לא תנאף״, and here it is written ״אל תחלל את בתך״. ״לא תגנוב״, and here it is written ״לא תגנבו״. ״לא תענה״, and here it is written ״לא תלך רכיל״. ״לא תחמוד״, and here it is written ״ואהבת לרעך כמוך״. Thus far in the Midrash, and likewise in the Yerushalmi of Berakhot." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'You shall be holy' — the holy ones are drawn from the qedesh. And so we find at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 22): ״ואנשי קדש תהיון לי״. 'A man, his mother and his father you shall fear' — ḥokhmah and yir'ah; and therefore he placed 'his mother' first, in order to bring yir'ah close to 'the father,' which is 'the fear of HaShem is the beginning of ḥokhmah.' 'And My Sabbaths you shall keep' — זכור and שמור, which are the great Sabbath and her counterpart; and from this it did not say 'my Sabbath' (in the singular). 'I HaShem your Elohim' — all is unified without severance or division."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:32.680506852Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}